Write zeros to a used recurrent state row before a new sequence starts in it
Fold-into: upstream/memory

The defects. tests/fuzz/core/run.sh test replays the reproducer of each one.

- A new sequence starts from NaN when its recurrent cell held a NaN.

  The graph gives a new sequence its first state from row rs_z, which it clears with a scale by 0
  (build_rs). A NaN or an Inf in that row stays NaN, because NaN * 0 and Inf * 0 are NaN. A state
  blob can load with success and hold NaN values (the load cannot see them), and a decode that
  overflows writes NaN. A remove of the sequence and llama_memory_clear(mem, false) keep the data
  of the cell. Thus each later sequence that starts in that cell gives NaN logits, until a clear
  with data.

  The change: The recurrent memory records for each cell whether the first group of its row holds
  zeros: true after the allocation, a clear with data, or a zero write, and false after a decode or
  a load of the cell. When a ubatch starts a new sequence (rs_z >= 0) in a row that is not zero,
  the memory context writes zeros to that row on the host (one ggml_backend_tensor_set for each
  state tensor of each layer, from a zero row that it keeps), before the graph runs. The graph and
  its ops do not change. A steady decode has no rs_z, and a new chat after a clear with data, a
  restore of a state and the MTP draft context (a plain KV cache) write nothing.

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_recurrent--state-nan-reuse.

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 with the patches of patches/series above this one.

